-- A settlement the payment rail told us about, and that we recorded hearing about.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand, like
-- `0011_material_odin.sql` and `0012_outstanding_flatman.sql`. It is the only SQL
-- this feature ships.
--
-- ## The hole this table closes
--
-- `POST /v1/loyalty/checkins` exists and works: a merchant's terminal reports a
-- settled sale, Vyloop stores it, and the tenant's earn rule turns
-- `amount_cents` into points. What nothing could see was the case where **that
-- report never arrives**.
--
-- The sale still happened. The customer paid. And because the report is the only
-- event in this system that says a loyalty pass was scanned -- the payment rail
-- carries the payment and the amount, and knows nothing about the pass -- a
-- missing report produces no row anywhere. The customer accumulated nothing and
-- nothing knew.
--
-- `scripts/check-checkin-backlog.mjs` cannot see it. That script reports sales
-- recorded *without points*; a sale that was never reported has no row for it to
-- report on. So this table is what makes the question answerable at all:
-- "the rail says payment X settled and no `checkins` row names it" is a join.
--
-- ## What this table deliberately does NOT hold
--
-- The raw webhook body. This is a reconciliation key set, not an event log. The
-- payload is written to `wrangler tail` by whoever is debugging it, and it carries
-- whatever the rail chose to send -- line items, payer details -- none of which any
-- check here reads. Storing what the check needs keeps the PII out of the row that
-- outlives the incident, and means a rail that adds a field does not silently start
-- persisting it here.
--
-- ## Why `payment_id` alone is the primary key
--
-- A payment id is the rail's own identifier for a settlement and is unique across
-- the platform. This is deliberately **not** scoped to `business_id` the way
-- `uq_checkins_business_payment` is.
--
-- `checkins` is written by whoever reports a sale, so the tenant has to be in its
-- key to make one tenant's retried `payment_id` idempotent against another's. This
-- row is written once, by the rail, for a payment belonging to exactly one
-- merchant -- and putting `business_id` in the key would make a duplicate delivery
-- insertable *twice* if the merchant reference were ever resolved wrongly, once
-- per tenant.
--
-- The honest cost: a mis-resolved `business_id` is a row attributed to the wrong
-- merchant, and the primary key cannot prevent it. What prevents it is resolving
-- the reference against `tenants` rather than reading a `business_id` out of the
-- body, which is why the route does a lookup instead. Stated here rather than
-- papered over.
--
-- ## Why there is no index on this table
--
-- The only queries against it are operational -- the reconciliation join against
-- `checkins` and a per-merchant rollup -- both `scan`-shaped over a table holding
-- one row per settled payment. An index here would be a write cost paid on every
-- delivery to speed up a query a human runs.
--
-- This is the same reasoning `0012_outstanding_flatman.sql` records for
-- `pos_terminals`, and it carries the same consequence: when an index does land it
-- has to touch **two** files -- the extra-config callback in `schema.ts`, **and**
-- `INDEXED_TABLES` in `indexes.ts` -- because `migration-sql.test.ts` asserts that
-- the `CREATE INDEX` statements across this history are exactly what that registry
-- reports. Declaring one without registering it there fails the build.
--
-- ## What is NOT here
--
-- **No reconciliation route, and no poll.** The endpoint that writes this table is
-- `POST /v1/loyalty/webhooks/payments` in `workers/growth/src/routes.ts`; the check
-- that reads it is `scripts/check-payment-reconciliation.mjs`. Neither credits a
-- point, creates a checkin, or resolves a customer, and neither can: the webhook
-- carries the payment and the amount, and only the terminal knows a pass was
-- scanned.
--
-- So this table detects a lost attribution and cannot repair one. Repair needs the
-- terminal to resend `POST /v1/loyalty/checkins`, or a person to ask the cashier.
--
-- **No Mercado Pago client, and no credentials.** No polling client exists in this
-- repository and none is written here; the other developer is building the rail
-- integration. A poll is explicitly deferred, because a webhook receiver that also
-- polls is two sources of truth for "did we hear about this payment", and only one
-- of them would be writing this table.
--
-- **No row-level security.** Consistent with `0004_chubby_crystal.sql`, which
-- asserts it: enabling RLS from a migration applies tenant isolation to every table
-- in one shot, and the workers do not set the session context on every code path.
-- `settled_payments` *is* registered in `BUSINESS_ID_TABLES`, so it receives a
-- policy when the separate, reviewed RLS rollout runs -- that rollout is this
-- table's isolation story, and it must not arrive by accident in a CREATE TABLE.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A person
-- applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
CREATE TABLE "settled_payments" (
	"payment_id" text PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
	"business_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"amount_cents" integer NOT NULL,
	"received_at" timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT NULL
);
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "settled_payments" ADD CONSTRAINT "settled_payments_business_id_tenants_id_fk" FOREIGN KEY ("business_id") REFERENCES "public"."tenants"("id") ON DELETE cascade ON UPDATE no action;